for this shopkeeper in dakar the  presidential inauguration of alfa conde  is another step back for democracy in  his home country guinea  mustafa bade looks concerned as he  watches the head of guinea's  constitutional court  praised the 86 year old president's  eagerness to take on a third term  he says he's not duped and has kept a  tally of all that has gone wrong since  kante came to power ten years ago  from tweaking the constitution to allow  him to stay in power  to rampant corruption and arresting  opponents in a country rich in minerals  like gold and bauxite  most ghanaians are trapped in poverty  baldi says  conde is failing his people at the  beginning we thought alfa conde would  bring us hope  but he has shown us his true colors he  has disappointed us  he doesn't respect the constitution and  he exercises control of  everything people are scared of him they  are either getting arrested  or killed after his victory was  announced in october the military fired  live bullets at protesters and went  house to house detaining mostly people  from the  fulani ethnic group who support  opposition candidate celut dalengalo  he believes conde rigged the elections  and says he won the vote  the inauguration is taking place as  protests are being held  gathered during the ceremony are a  number of aging african heads of state  who like conde have changed their  country's constitution to stay in power  outside of baldi's shop are young  african migrants from  various french-speaking african  countries all of them share this common  feeling that they've left behind  nations led by aging presidents clinging  on to power  robbing their nation's wealth and  leaving young people without jobs  opportunities and the space and freedom  to express their political views at the  cars museum of black civilization the  activists from across the continent have  gathered  to warn of a dangerous trend of civil  liberties being stifled  and governments muffling democracy and  freedom of expression among those taking  part  is ibrahimovialo an outspoken critic of  conde conde's government  has issued an international arrest  warrant against him  initially  we have seen the nation state use the  global coronavirus pandemic as an excuse  to restrict individual  and collective freedoms of citizens  banning protests when these do not suit  governments  this pandemic is benefiting autocratic  regimes  the borders between guinea and senegal  are closed valde is unable to return  home to his  family and with the inauguration of  alphaconde  he fears there will be more trouble  ahead nicholas hawk  al jazeera dakar
 

